module v2.std.witness_evaluation


data witness_evaluation_frame_note: String = "A witness evaluation is an ordinary scoped evaluation whose effects are supplied by an existing Envelope. Envelope already owns its Frame, so this carrier does not duplicate a separately writable frame field. The REST fixtures are temporary realization inputs selected through the envelope's HandlerBinding; they are not global interpreter state and must disappear when subject evaluation returns or refuses. This frame is deliberately not REST-specific in its result or control semantics: later deterministic environment and filesystem replay bindings extend the same carrier rather than minting another catch primitive."

type WitnessEvaluationFrame {
  envelope: std.effect_grant.Envelope
  rest_fixtures: List<extdeps.transports.rest.RestExchangeFixture>
}

type WitnessEvaluation<A>
  = WitnessReturned { value: A }
  | WitnessRefused { diagnostic: v2.std.diagnostic.Diagnostic }

data evaluate_in_witness_frame_seed_note: String = "The v1 interpreter realizes this generic boundary by installing the frame for the dynamic extent of subject(), returning its value or converting the interpreter refusal to a located Diagnostic, and removing the frame on both paths. The authored body is the pure success projection for emitted targets until their effect-handler/evaluation-frame realization lands. There are no test cases or REST judgments in the Rust realization; all fixture facts and assertions remain authored .dag values.\\n\\nSEED-RETENTION RECEIPT (review 46616), stated so the deferral is checkable rather than open-ended. LANE: ROADMAP `v1-materialization-kernel` (docs/plans/witness-realization-plan.md), counted against `v1-honest-frontier` and terminating at `v1-interpreter-quarantine` then `v1-interpreter-delete`. WHAT IS DEFERRED, exactly: the dynamic-extent install/remove of a frame, and nothing else - the policy this boundary exists to serve is modeled here and in extdeps.transports.rest, and the interpreter calls rest_exchange_resolution back into .dag rather than deciding replay itself. DELETION CONDITION, by execution: witnesses emit to native code and the emitted runtime realizes the frame, at which point the seed's frame stack and its dispatch delete together and rest_replay_binding_does_not_escape_its_frame stays green without them. That witness is the deletion's regression control, not merely the frame's.\\n\\nTHE TWO STUBS ARE A SEPARATE, SMALLER DEBT AND MUST NOT HIDE INSIDE THE LANE ABOVE. witness_diagnostic_rendered_reason returns the empty string and evaluate_in_witness_frame always answers WitnessReturned when no interpreter realizes them, so under a pure evaluator a refusal is indistinguishable from a success carrying an empty reason. That is fail-open in the direction that matters, and it is why every witness consuming this boundary today runs on the interpreted path where the realization exists. DISSOLVE-ON, and it is the nearer trigger: Diagnostic gains a modeled rendering so the reason projection is authored rather than realized, and the pure body becomes a refusal rather than a fabricated success. Until then a consumer that needs the refusal arm must execute on the realized path; a consumer that merely needs the value is unaffected."

data unrealized_frame_refusal_note: String = "THE PURE BODY REFUSES; IT DOES NOT FABRICATE A SUCCESS. This previously returned WitnessReturned unconditionally, so an evaluator without the frame realization reported every refusal as a success - the fabricated-plausible-output failure §5 forbids, sitting in the one function whose entire job is to report whether a subject returned or refused. It was defended as scaffolding with a dissolve-on, but a declared trigger does not make a fail-open arm fail closed; it only schedules its removal (review 46769).\n\nREFUSING COSTS NOTHING THAT WAS REAL. The interpreter dispatches this boundary by module and name and never evaluates this body, so the realized path is unchanged and the nine replay witnesses prove it by execution. What changes is the unrealized path: it now answers WitnessRefused with a located reason naming the missing realization, so a consumer that reaches it learns that no frame was installed instead of being told its subject succeeded. The frame's absence is the fact, and the refusal states it.\n\nSTILL OPEN, SEPARATELY: witness_diagnostic_rendered_reason returns the empty string under a pure evaluator. That is a weaker defect than this one was - it can no longer disguise a refusal as a success, only render a real refusal's reason as empty - and it dissolves on the same trigger: Diagnostic gains a modeled rendering so the projection is authored rather than realized."

fn witness_frame_unrealized_diagnostic() -> v2.std.diagnostic.Diagnostic {
  v2.std.diagnostic.Diagnostic {
    reason: ^witness_evaluation_frame_not_realized,
    at: v2.std.diagnostic.Textual { file: ^witness_evaluation_unrealized_boundary, extent: WholeFile },
    correction: v2.std.diagnostic.Unavailable { reason: AmbiguousIntent }
  }
}

fn evaluate_in_witness_frame<A>(
  frame: WitnessEvaluationFrame,
  subject: fn(Bool) -> A,
) -> WitnessEvaluation<A> {
  WitnessRefused { diagnostic: witness_frame_unrealized_diagnostic() }
}

// An executing-scope observation proves that a temporary frame was removed. The pure/default answer is false; the interpreter realization returns true only while evaluate_in_witness_frame is applying its subject.
fn witness_evaluation_frame_active() -> Bool {
  false
}

data witness_diagnostic_rendered_reason_note: String = "Diagnostic.reason remains a Symbol in the shared diagnostic authority. This generic projection lets a witness compare an interpreter's full rendered refusal without weakening that authority to an arbitrary String carrier."

fn witness_diagnostic_rendered_reason(diagnostic: v2.std.diagnostic.Diagnostic) -> String {
  ""
}
